<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 18pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;VTB_Example&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight&amp;nbsp;Technologies SystemVue Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;This workspace provides a few simple VTB (Verification Test Bench) designs that demonstrate the basic VTB features.&amp;nbsp;They are provided just for demonstration purposes and running them does NOT require any license other than the basic SystemVue one&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;None of the designs in this workspace will simulate in SystemVue because of the use of the SVE_Link model (the intent is to simulate them using SystemVue Engine in a cosimulation environment with another simulator, e.g. ADS or Golden Gate). To simulate them inside SystemVue, open the associated analysis and select the DF_Amp configuration.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;For more information on how to create VBT designs please refer to the following documentation sections:&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;Simulation &amp;gt; Verification Test Bench (VTB)&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt; mso-pagination: widow-orphan lines-together&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; FONT-FAMILY: &apos;Arial&apos;,&apos;sans-serif&apos;; LINE-HEIGHT: 115%; mso-fareast-font-family: &apos;Times New Roman&apos;&quot;&gt;Tutorials &amp;gt; Verification Test Bench Tutorial&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;The VTB designs included in this workspace are:&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;U&gt;EVM_Measurement&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;This VTB design performs an EVM measurement on a modulated signal. The symbol rate and modulation format of the&amp;nbsp;signal can be set through the VTB parameters. The modulator output power as well as certain modulation impairments can also be set using the VTB parameters.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;When adding impairments&amp;nbsp;(IQ Offset, Gain and Phase Imbalance) the constellation plot (EVM_Meas_Tx_Constellation) may seem inconsistent with the trajectory one (EVM_Meas_Tx_Trajectory). For example, applying an offset only along the x-axis might show (in the constellation plot) as offset along the other axis, both axes, or the expected axis but in the&amp;nbsp;opposite direction. The reason is that the constellation plot uses data generated by the EVM sink, whereas the trajectory plot uses data created by just filtering the signal with a Root Raised Cosine filter. The EVM sink performs demodulation on the input signal and may arbitrarily rotate it by any angle that is an integer multiple of the angle to which the constellation is phase invariant (rectangular QAM constellations are 90-degree phase invariant and Star-QAM constellations are 45-degree phase invariant).&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;U&gt;QAM16_SER_vs_EbNo&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;U&gt;&lt;/U&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;This VTB design uses the BER_IS model to efficiently compute a BER waterfall curve for a 16-QAM system. VTB parameters include the signal symbol rate, modulator output power, and range of SNR values over which BER is computed.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;U&gt;ACPR_Meaasurement&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;This VTB design demonstrates how to post-process simulation data in a VTB design.&amp;nbsp; It measures ACPR for a modulated signal. The symbol rate and the modulation format of the signal can be set using VTB parameters. The modulator output power is also set using a VTB parameter. The signal at the output of the modulator&amp;nbsp;goes through a non-linear amplifier whose output 1 dB compression point is a VTB parameter.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;ACPR is measured by computing the spectrum power in different bands using a custom ACPR function defined inside the SystemVue workspace. &lt;STRONG&gt;This ACPR function is for demonstration purposes only. It does&amp;nbsp;NOT comply with any standard. &lt;/STRONG&gt;Four user defined variables have been created in the dataset (MyACPRMinus1_dB, MyACPRMinus2_dB, MyACPRPlus1_dB, MyACPRPlus2_dB) that are calculated by calling the ACPR function. This function takes as arguments data created by the simulation (S2_Power, and S2_Power_Freq) as well as values computed based on the top level design parameters (FCarrier, SymbolRate). To get these parameters in the dataset you need to check the &lt;EM&gt;Save Design Parameters to Dataset&lt;/EM&gt; check box in the &lt;EM&gt;Options&lt;/EM&gt; tab of the Data Flow analysis.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;U&gt;How to use this workspace&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Use the example SVETester application to open this workspace and observe how the parameters of the VTB designs are presented in the SVETester GUI.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Change parameter values in the SVETester UI and run the simulation. Import the simulation results back into SystemVue (File &amp;gt; Import &amp;gt; ADX File...) and compare them with the results of running the same simulation inside SystemVue (to run the simulation inside SystemVue you need to select the DF_Amp configuration in the Data Flow Analysis dialog).&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
